{"skill":{"slug":"proactive-agent","displayName":"Proactive Agent"},"version":{"version":"3.1.0","createdAt":1770259214202,"changelog":"Added: Autonomous vs Prompted Crons, Verify Implementation Not Intent, Tool Migration Checklist","changelogSource":"user","license":null,"files":[{"path":"SKILL-v2.3-backup.md","size":20156,"sha256":"44bc35db30b536f8254906b8d2f65d366d046a372e801fbd542b30529de892e2","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"SKILL-v3-draft.md","size":16960,"sha256":"5a61dae55820c670d23de61500aa48956b7a1b20c3f01cdc7e596b51fd854c03","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"SKILL.md","size":20883,"sha256":"571521d9f6f38b3ec9f073d7bdec47170a35e9c78556954ac7f0f860ab12873b","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"assets/AGENTS.md","size":3860,"sha256":"f771f02d7ca86961c32afa1d62a2a39dbf7bd4994d2f2a59371818dad752edd1","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"assets/HEARTBEAT.md","size":2939,"sha256":"0412eed7f74c4171a46389d2084ce453612af5ebd5f5cd53b1b37ef44182c277","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"assets/MEMORY.md","size":836,"sha256":"44153b7be3fb08d12f7cb4d8488b07fe9829b677f2103a5b372d83b64a96dbe7","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"assets/ONBOARDING.md","size":2423,"sha256":"3227d5131537f1942c039c991aaa120d20b600001d94b6cfd92c6c54b545ca25","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"assets/SOUL.md","size":1431,"sha256":"25bfe585259fa09e70e843964bfa159a2ddb02bbaced14ba4c7ce845e8f61a7b","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"assets/TOOLS.md","size":1044,"sha256":"8801752bfd4e0efe0f05b04785766e32d61218d4571afe36d0833539e6ea94ba","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"assets/USER.md","size":926,"sha256":"2d9649f7975bc332d343cf2a2b0809cb5c361ac0002e1e42e6030b09b3f2b261","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"references/onboarding-flow.md","size":4078,"sha256":"d6ca324a7819a10183b13965abeefe91de5f854f46288c4b8216fe3d246168e1","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"references/security-patterns.md","size":3034,"sha256":"0e2024a6e11d48add2ce5c820e79cf048dc656a8e0458ff7acf4419881a0ab63","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"scripts/security-audit.sh","size":4110,"sha256":"68c2669f4c11d3a7a930b8fee07029b51dcf98902841e46e286c0772fc9ca429","contentType":"application/x-sh"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2868,"sha256":"add90e604f470ec24170a9f307756e046a35cc8d04bbfb97a2555fc9bc00adac","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789023198520,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"fa8eec1c245f6976193e456827e5dda9f434ff951bddf43a9cf5477751c3bc24","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/fa8eec1c245f6976193e456827e5dda9f434ff951bddf43a9cf5477751c3bc24","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"clean","verdict":"benign","normalizedStatus":"clean","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: proactive-agent\nVersion: 3.1.0\n\nThis skill bundle is designed to create a proactive, self-improving AI agent with extensive security hardening. Multiple files (SKILL.md, AGENTS.md, SOUL.md, references/security-patterns.md) contain explicit instructions for the agent to defend against prompt injection, prevent data exfiltration, confirm deletions, and vet other skills for malicious content. The included `scripts/security-audit.sh` actively scans for exposed secrets, insecure permissions, and checks for security best practices. While the agent is instructed to use broad capabilities like CLI access, web search, and cron jobs, these are consistently framed within the context of problem-solving for the human, with strong guardrails against unauthorized external actions. There is no evidence of intentional harmful behavior; rather, the content demonstrates a strong focus on security and safe operation.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779160234063},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":100,"severity":"CRITICAL","recommendation":"DO_NOT_INSTALL","issueCount":73,"checkedAt":1789023198520},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"This skill is openly meant to make an agent proactive and persistent, but it gives the agent broad automatic memory, self-modification, bootstrap, background-work, and cleanup authority without enough user control.","dimensions":[{"detail":"The requested environment behavior includes broad local memory search, periodic checks of email/calendar/logs/projects, spawning autonomous isolated agent turns, and desktop/browser cleanup suggestions, which is powerful for the stated purpose but insufficiently bounded.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"Installation is documented as copying Markdown assets into the workspace and optionally running a local audit script; the script is mostly read-only, but the security-audit behavior is only lightly surfaced in quick start and the credential template claims .credentials is gitignored without shipping a .gitignore.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The skill treats mutable workspace files as operational instructions, tells the agent to follow and delete BOOTSTRAP.md if present, and encourages immediate updates to AGENTS.md, TOOLS.md, or skill files after lessons learned, creating an under-scoped persistent instruction channel.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"Persistent storage is central to the design, but it lacks clear opt-in boundaries, redaction rules, retention limits, permission guidance for memory files, and approval before promoting ordinary conversation or third-party details into durable memory.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The proactive-agent purpose matches much of the package, including memory, onboarding, security hardening, and proactive check-ins, but the artifacts go further by directing automatic capture of names, preferences, decisions, URLs, and every exchange after a context threshold into persistent files.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"concern"}],"guidance":"Install only if you want a highly persistent, proactive agent and are comfortable auditing the generated memory and governance files. Before use, disable automatic full-exchange logging, require approval before writing personal or third-party details to memory, remove the BOOTSTRAP auto-follow/delete rule, require approval before modifying AGENTS.md or similar policy files, add a real .gitignore before storing credentials, and turn heartbeat cleanup into a proposed-action report rather than automatic app/tab/file cleanup.","findings":"[SSD-3] unexpected: The WAL and working-buffer behavior is related to persistence, but the artifact-backed rule to log every qualifying user detail and every exchange after 60% context is broader than necessary and lacks consent or minimization controls.\n[SQP-2] unexpected: Onboarding persistence is partly disclosed, but the artifacts also say skipped or informal onboarding still fills USER.md from natural conversation, which creates a retention transparency gap.\n[T01] unexpected: The persistent instruction concern is supported: AGENTS.md says to follow then delete BOOTSTRAP.md, read local governance/memory files before doing anything, and update instruction-bearing files immediately after lessons learned.\n[T05] unexpected: Heartbeat cleanup is supported by HEARTBEAT.md instructions to close apps, close browser tabs, and move old screenshots to trash; this conflicts with the stricter confirmation-before-deletion rule and lacks transaction-specific approval.\n[T09] unexpected: The credential-storage concern is supported: TOOLS.md states .credentials is gitignored, but the artifact package does not include a .gitignore, and the permission guidance in references/security-patterns.md is imprecise for directories.\n[TP4] expected: The local security-audit script fits the security-hardening portion of the skill and appears read-only, so this is downgraded to a disclosure/usability note rather than an independent high-severity concern.\n[P1] expected: The prompt-injection phrases are examples of attacks to detect, not instructions for the agent to obey or hidden directives.\n[P2] expected: The HTML comment prompt-injection text appears inside a security-patterns example of indirect injections, not as an active hidden instruction.\n[YR4] expected: The matched strings are part of prompt-injection detection guidance or general agent-description text; they do not show artifact-backed exploit code or malicious command behavior.\n[P7] expected: The term reverse prompting is used to mean proactive suggestion and question generation, not system-prompt extraction.\n[PE3] expected: The .env and .credentials references occur in a read-only audit that checks gitignore coverage and permissions; this does not read or transmit secret values.\n[other] unexpected: The excessive personal and conversation data retention concern is accepted because the main skill and templates instruct broad automatic capture into persistent workspace files without clear retention or sensitive-data boundaries.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789023198520}}}}}